# Climate AI: environment for docker compose.
#
#   make bootstrap        # or scripts/bootstrap.sh
#
# copies this file to .env (mode 600), generates every empty secret below (never touching one
# that is already set, and adding any that an older .env lacks), starts the stack and prints
# the URL. Doing it by hand: cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env, then fill in every secret
# (docs/DEPLOY.md, "Configure"). NEVER commit .env: it holds the database password, the key
# that decrypts your ecobee token and HomeKit pairing keys, the sign-in secrets and your Claude
# sign-in token.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line, no spaces around '='. Avoid '$' and quotes in values
# (or wrap the value in single quotes). Generated values below are hex or URL-safe base64.
#
# ##########################################################################################
# #  NEVER set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (or ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK/VERTEX)  #
# #  anywhere on this server: not here, not in your shell profile. It takes priority over   #
# #  your Claude subscription and bills the API per token. Claude runs on your plan via     #
# #  CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN below. The agent refuses to start if it finds one.             #
# ##########################################################################################

# --- Compose ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional profiles to start with a plain `docker compose up -d` (comma separated):
#   homekit (local HomeKit, Linux host only), mcp (MCP server), ntfy (push notifications)
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=homekit
# Only if your nginx runs in Docker (see docker-compose.nginx.yml). For the GrowWise AppRelay
# gateway (public name, docs/PUBLIC_ACCESS.md) the network is apprelay_gateway; for another
# nginx container, find its network with `docker network ls`.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.nginx.yml
# NGINX_NETWORK=apprelay_gateway

# Container time zone (e.g. America/Chicago), used for log timestamps only; the house time
# zone that drives schedules is set in the Setup page. bootstrap copies this computer's zone
# into a new .env.
TZ=UTC

# --- Database (TimescaleDB) -----------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=climate
# Required. URL-safe please (it is embedded in a connection URL): openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=climate
# Host port for Postgres, bound to 127.0.0.1 only. The host-networked homekit service (and
# `make homekit-native` on a Mac) connects here. 5433 avoids a Postgres already on the host;
# if something else holds it (GrowWise's native Postgres uses 5433 too), bootstrap picks the
# next free port and writes it here.
DB_PORT=5433

# The app/worker connection URL is derived from the POSTGRES_* values above
# (postgresql+psycopg://USER:PASSWORD@db:5432/DB, compose-internal host `db`).
# Set this only to use a different database; the homekit service always uses
# 127.0.0.1:${DB_PORT}.
# CLIMATE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://climate:CHANGE_ME@db:5432/climate

# --- Web app port ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The app listens on ${APP_BIND}:${APP_PORT}. Keep 127.0.0.1 behind a reverse proxy (host
# nginx, or the GrowWise gateway through docker-compose.nginx.yml). APP_BIND=0.0.0.0 opens it
# to your home network over plain http (phones on Wi-Fi; `scripts/bootstrap.sh --lan` sets
# it): then keep CLIMATE_COOKIE_SECURE=false and choose the owner password right away, since
# until then anyone on your network could choose it. Never port-forward this port.
# bootstrap moves APP_PORT to the next free port if something else holds it.
APP_BIND=127.0.0.1
APP_PORT=8470
# `make dev` only (docker-compose.dev.yml): where the live-reload API is published for the Vite
# dev server.
# DEV_API_PORT=8000

# --- Secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# bootstrap generates every one of these. By hand: `openssl rand -hex 32` for each, except the
# Fernet key (`openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_'`). Once the image is built,
#   docker run --rm climate-ai-app:local python -m climate.cli gen-key
# prints a Fernet key, a JWT secret and a pepper.
#
# Fernet key that encrypts the ecobee refresh token and the HomeKit pairing keys in the
# database. Required. BACK IT UP SEPARATELY from the database backups. Without it the stored
# ecobee sign-in and HomeKit pairings cannot be decrypted (and every thermostat needs a
# HomeKit reset to re-pair).
CLIMATE_SECRET_KEY=
# Signs the 15-minute access tokens (HS256). Required, at least 32 characters. Changing it
# only ends the current access tokens; signed-in devices renew theirs silently.
CLIMATE_JWT_SECRET=
# HMAC key for every stored token hash (signed-in devices' refresh tokens, API tokens).
# Required, at least 32 characters. Changing it signs every device out and voids every API
# token.
CLIMATE_TOKEN_PEPPER=
# Optional: the owner password, hashed (Argon2id) into the database on the first start and
# ignored after that. Leave empty and choose it on the first screen from your home network, or
# with `make password`. If you use it, delete it from this file after the first start.
CLIMATE_OWNER_PASSWORD=
# Service tokens for the agent and the MCP server (role "agent": read, propose, sign off;
# never a thermostat write, the controller mode, settings or tokens). Accepted only from
# private addresses (home network, Docker, Tailscale). Instead of these you can create a
# revocable "cai_..." token in the app (More > Security > API tokens, role Agent) and paste it
# here. Any other service (a dashboard, a script) gets its own API token from that page.
CLIMATE_AGENT_TOKEN=
CLIMATE_MCP_TOKEN=

# --- Behaviour ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data source on first start: simulator (no credentials needed) or ecobee. After the first
# start the Setup page decides; this value is only the initial default.
CLIMATE_SOURCE=simulator
# Marks the sign-in (refresh) cookie Secure. true whenever the app is reached over HTTPS
# (behind nginx or the GrowWise gateway); over plain http browsers drop Secure cookies and
# sign-in does not stick, so bootstrap sets false for local http use. `make doctor` flags an
# https CLIMATE_PUBLIC_URL with this still false.
CLIMATE_COOKIE_SECURE=true
# Public base URL used in notification links, e.g. https://climate.apprelay.net
CLIMATE_PUBLIC_URL=
# INFO, or DEBUG while troubleshooting (the ecobee library's token logging stays off either way).
CLIMATE_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Advanced (defaults shown; uncomment to change):
# ecobee summary polling, seconds (ecobee asks for no faster than every 3 minutes; min 180)
# CLIMATE_POLL_SECONDS=180
# Simulator polling, seconds, and days of synthetic history generated on first start
# CLIMATE_SIM_POLL_SECONDS=60
# CLIMATE_SIM_BACKFILL_DAYS=60
# CLIMATE_SIM_SEED=7
# ecobee's own web client id for account sign-in (unofficial route; change only if it breaks)
# CLIMATE_ECOBEE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=183eORFPlXyz9BbDZwqexHPBQoVjgadh
# HomeKit service state directory inside the container (charmap cache only)
# CLIMATE_HOMEKIT_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/climate/homekit

# --- Sign-in (advanced; defaults shown, docs/specs/users-and-tokens.md) ---------------------
# Access token lifetime; signed-in device (refresh) lifetime, sliding; hard cap from sign-in
# CLIMATE_ACCESS_TTL_MINUTES=15
# CLIMATE_REFRESH_TTL_DAYS=30
# CLIMATE_SESSION_MAX_DAYS=90
# How recently the password must have been re-entered to create an API token
# CLIMATE_REAUTH_WINDOW_MINUTES=10
# Minimum length for a new owner password
# CLIMATE_PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=10
# Failed sign-ins from PUBLIC addresses (counted together): past this many in the window,
# sign-in from the internet pauses (from home and Tailscale it always works)
# CLIMATE_PUBLIC_LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=20
# CLIMATE_PUBLIC_LOGIN_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# CLIMATE_LOGIN_PAUSE_MINUTES=15
# DANGER: lets the first-run screen choose the owner password from ANY address. Keep false;
# choose the password from home (or with `make password`) before the app is reachable from
# the internet.
# CLIMATE_ALLOW_REMOTE_SETUP=false

# --- Push notifications (ntfy) --------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty URL disables push. With the ntfy profile: CLIMATE_NTFY_URL=http://ntfy
# Or use the public server: CLIMATE_NTFY_URL=https://ntfy.sh with a long random topic.
CLIMATE_NTFY_URL=
# Topic name. On a public server, anyone who knows it can read it: make it unguessable,
# e.g. climate-$(openssl rand -hex 8)
# Required to enable push. Make it long and random, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 16`: anyone who
# knows a topic on a public ntfy server can read and post to it.
CLIMATE_NTFY_TOPIC=
# Access token if your ntfy server requires auth (tk_...)
CLIMATE_NTFY_TOKEN=
# Only for the self-hosted ntfy profile: the URL your phone uses to reach it, and the
# iPhone push relay (opt-in; see docker-compose.yml).
# NTFY_BASE_URL=https://ntfy.example.home
# NTFY_UPSTREAM_BASE_URL=https://ntfy.sh
# NTFY_PORT=8472

# --- Claude (agent service, your Claude subscription) ----------------------------------------
# 1. On any computer with a browser and Claude Code installed, run:   claude setup-token
#    Approve in the browser; it prints a long-lived token (valid for one year).
# 2. Paste it below and record today's date (the app warns 30 days before it expires).
# 3. docker compose up -d agent
# Leave empty to run without Claude: the agent idles and the app shows "not signed in".
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# Date the token was created, YYYY-MM-DD
CLAUDE_TOKEN_CREATED=
# Optional overrides (defaults: claude-opus-5-5, falling back to claude-sonnet-5-5; turn cap 30)
# CLIMATE_AGENT_MODEL=claude-opus-5-5
# CLIMATE_AGENT_FALLBACK_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
# CLIMATE_AGENT_MAX_TURNS=30

# --- MCP server (profile mcp) -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Bind address for port ${MCP_PORT}. 127.0.0.1 = usable from the server itself or through an
# SSH tunnel. Set to the server's Tailscale IP to reach it from your laptop over Tailscale.
# MCP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# MCP_PORT=8471

# --- Reverse proxy ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Addresses whose X-Forwarded-For uvicorn trusts. It decides who counts as "home" (first-run
# setup, home-network-only API tokens, the legacy service tokens) and which address the
# sign-in throttle counts. Default: Docker bridge networks. With nginx in Docker on a shared
# network (the GrowWise gateway), set this to the nginx container's IP, or at least that
# network's subnet, so other containers on it cannot pretend to be someone else.
# FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS=172.16.0.0/12

# --- Outbound HTTP proxy ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only if your network forces an HTTP proxy (Docker then injects HTTP(S)_PROXY into every
# container). Compose already exempts the internal names (app, db, ntfy, localhost); add more
# exceptions here, comma separated, e.g. a LAN ntfy server:
# EXTRA_NO_PROXY=ntfy.home.lan,192.168.1.20
